            "id": "pte99-r-mcm-1",
            "passageText": "Dendrochronology uses annual tree-ring growth layers to date climatic events and ancient wooden artifacts. In temperate climates, trees form one ring per year, with ring width reflecting environmental moisture and temperature conditions. Wide growth rings signal favorable wet growing seasons, while narrow rings record severe drought. By cross-matching ring sequences from living trees with timber from historical structures, researchers construct continuous climate chronologies spanning millennia.",
